# viola-deno-runtime: Design

`cdylib` that bridges TypeScript / Deno lint projects into the viola plugin ABI. The crate exports a single `ExtensionDescriptor` whose providers wrap calls to a Deno worker subprocess that runs the TS lint logic. From the viola host's perspective this is one plugin; from the Deno side it is a worker that loads the consumer's TS module.

## Provider implementations

The crate ships one provider for each of the three viola roles, so a single Deno-backed plugin can act as runner, grammar, and lint.

### `RunnerProvider`

Wraps the Deno worker for `viola.runner.execute_scope.v1`. Marshals `RunScope` across the subprocess boundary, receives the produced `NamPayload` back.

### `GrammarProvider`

Wraps the Deno worker for `viola.grammar.extract.v1`. Forwards source bytes; receives a contribution `NamPayload`.

### `LintEvalProvider`

Wraps the Deno worker for `viola.lint.evaluate.v1`. Forwards `NamPayload` + lint config; receives `DiagnosticBatch`.

## Lifecycle

### `InitImpl`

`InitHandler` impl that spawns the Deno worker subprocess and establishes the request channel.

### `ShutdownImpl`

`ShutdownHandler` impl that closes the channel and waits for the worker to exit cleanly.

## Extension entry point

### `DenoRuntimeExtension`

The descriptor-shaped marker. The `#[export_extension]` macro on this struct emits the `__hilavitkutin_extension_descriptor` exported function plus the per-provider trampolines.

## IPC layer

The bridge spawns `deno` via `std::process::Command` and does not embed `deno_core`. Cross-process payloads marshal across the subprocess boundary through a set of crate-private carriers plus a small per-call arena that stages payloads before the channel write. The carriers are not part of the consumer surface; they live as internal scaffolding the bridge uses to encode and decode IPC frames.

### `BridgeMessage`

Wire envelope the worker emits on stdout. Carries an optional diagnostic, an optional done flag, an optional error string. Read frame-by-frame on the host side.

### `BridgeDiag`

Diagnostic record as the worker emits it. Carries plugin id, rule id, severity, message, path, line, column. Converted to `viola_plugin_abi::Diagnostic` before host-side return.

### `LintRequest`

Request payload sent to the worker for one lint invocation. Carries the op tag plus the scope payload.

### `ScopePayload`

Run-scope info forwarded to the worker. Carries the workspace root and the file list.

### `FilePayload`

Per-file metadata inside `ScopePayload`. Carries the path and the language tag.

### `ShutdownRequest`

Terminate signal sent on shutdown.

### `Arena`

Plugin-side arena holding the owned bytes that a `Diagnostic` references via `BytesRef`. Rebuilt on each lint invocation so each call's pointers stay valid only until the next one, matching the v1 contract that "host copies before next invocation".

### `WorkerState`

Live worker handle. Holds the subprocess child, the stdin pipe, the stdout reader, the bridge script path, and the per-call `Arena`. Owned by the extension across the loaded library's lifetime; the `Drop` impl ensures the worker exits cleanly when the host unloads.

## Stack discipline

Per workspace primitive-vocabulary discipline, the IPC layer is moving off `serde` and `serde_json` toward hand-rolled stack-buffer encoding. The locked direction: drop `serde`, drop `serde_json`, drop `alloc`-backed `Vec`, encode IPC frames into fixed-size buffers in the per-call arena, and use `// lint:allow` markers only where the subprocess boundary genuinely cannot avoid the stack-discipline lint (the documented exemptions live with the markers).

The subprocess route avoids the much larger dependency surface that embedding `deno_core` would have required; the cost is the IPC encoding work the bridge now owns.

## Dependencies

Depends on `viola-plugin-abi` for the ABI surface, on `hilavitkutin-extensions` for the loader contract, on `hilavitkutin-extensions-macros` for `#[export_extension]`. Depends on `notko` and `arvo` for fallibility and numeric primitives.
